ed syllable (/əl/). (Count: 6)\n10. **to** (/tə/): Typically unstressed. (Count: 7)\n11. **explain** (/ɪkˈspleɪn/): Contains an unstressed syllable (/ɪk/). (Count: 8)\n12. **to** (/tə/): Typically unstressed. (Count: 9)\n13. **the** (/ðə/): Typically unstressed. (Count: 10)\n14. **child** (/tʃaɪld/): Content word, likely stressed. (Likely Stressed)\n15. **so** (/soʊ/): Can be stressed or unstressed. Here, it seems to function as an adverb/filler, likely carrying some stress or being neutral. (Uncertain)\n16. **to** (/tə/): Typically unstressed. (Count: 11)\n17. **speak** (/spiːk/): Content word, likely stressed. (Likely Stressed)\n18. **or** (/ɔːr/): Can be unstressed (/ər/). (Count: 12)\n19. **the** (/ðə/): Typically unstressed. (Count: 13)\n20. **child** (/tʃaɪld/): Content word, likely stressed. (Likely Stressed)\n\n**Words confirmed to have unstressed phonemes (function words or words with clear unstressed syllables):**\nwith, the, whereby, to, be, able, to, explain, to, the, to, or, the.\nTotal confirmed: 13\n\n**Words that are typically stressed (content words):**\nman, I, want, child, so, speak, child.\n\nIf we strictly count words that *must* contain an unstressed phoneme (like multi-syllable words or common unstressed function words):\n1. with (unstressed /ɪ/)\n2. the (unstressed /ə/)\n3. whereby (unstressed /wɛər/)\n4. to (unstressed /tə/)
